<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">State of Vietnam</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1949">1949</a>–<a href="/years/1955">1955</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> was the French-backed government of Vietnam headed by the former emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bao-dai">Bảo Đại</a>, created by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elysee-accords">Élysée Accords</a> of 1949 as an alternative to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a>. Highland territories such as the Tai Federation were nominally attached to it. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> Accords of 1954 it held the south, and in 1955 Ngô Đình Diệm deposed Bảo Đại and proclaimed the Republic of Vietnam.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/state-of-vietnam.jpg" alt="State of Vietnam" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: (many, see history) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_South_Vietnam.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/august-revolution">August Revolution</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a> declared the independence of the Democratic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Full-scale war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> began in December 1946. Surviving nationalists who opposed both the communists and French rule rallied behind the exiled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bao-dai">Bảo Đại</a>. On 5 June 1948 the Halong Bay Agreements allowed a unified Vietnamese government within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-union">French Union</a>, and a Provisional Central Government was proclaimed under Nguyễn Văn Xuân while Bảo Đại waited for full reunification.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On 20 May 1949 the French National Assembly approved the reunification of Cochinchina with the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Bảo Đại brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elysee-accords">Élysée Accords</a> into effect on 14 June 1949, and the first government of the State of Vietnam was proclaimed on 2 July. It had partial autonomy as an associated state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-union">French Union</a>, and it gained recognition from 34 countries by 1950, among them the United Kingdom and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Its strongest support came from the United States, while the Democratic Republic was backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and, from 1950, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>.</p><p>Roughly 60 per cent of Vietnamese territory was under the Democratic Republic in 1952, but most delta and urban areas with large populations were held by the French and the State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Five groups, known as the Big Five, chose to work with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bao-dai">Bảo Đại</a>: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caodaism">Cao Đài</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoa-hao">Hòa Hảo</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/binh-xuyen">Bình Xuyên</a>, the northern Catholics and the northern Đại Việt Party. Most kept semi-independent territories of their own. A Vietnamese National Army was created under General Nguyễn Văn Hinh, and a national currency was introduced in 1953.</p><p>The Matignon Treaty of 4 June 1954 was seen as granting independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Bảo Đại appointed Ngô Đình Diệm to lead the government that month, and on 20 July 1954 the state formally withdrew from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-union">French Union</a>. The Geneva Accords of 21 July divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> at the 17th parallel. Some 120,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> personnel moved north, while at least 500,000 Catholics and others moved south.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> opposed the partition. In July 1955 Diệm announced that the south would not take part in the elections the Geneva Accords specified, since it had not signed them. A referendum on 23 October 1955 chose between Diệm and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bao-dai">Bảo Đại</a>. Supporters of Bảo Đại were not allowed to campaign, the official result of 98.2 per cent for Diệm was condemned as fraudulent, and the votes counted exceeded the registered voters by over 380,000. On 26 October 1955 Diệm proclaimed the Republic of Vietnam.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “State of Vietnam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The radicalisation of the state provoked large-scale defections from the maquis to areas held by the French and the <b>State of Vietnam</b>, among them intellectuals, landlords, soldiers and cadres such as Phạm Duy and Nguyễn Văn Thiệu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/democratic-republic-of-vietnam">Democratic Republic of Vietnam · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>France granted limited independence to the <b>Associated States of Vietnam</b> under Bao Dai, Laos and Cambodia in 1949, and Cambodia obtained full independence in 1953.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Republic of Vietnam grew out of the <b>State of Vietnam</b>, created on 14 June 1949 through co-operation between anti-communist Vietnamese and the French during the First Indochina War, with the former emperor Bảo Đại as chief of state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-vietnam">Republic of Vietnam · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
